---
import BlogArticle from '../../components/BlogArticle.astro';
import BlogCode from '../../components/BlogCode.astro';

const contents = [
  { id: 'tuesday', label: 'An idea on Tuesday, access on Friday' },
  { id: 'order', label: 'Reverse the order' },
  { id: 'try-it', label: 'A working project in a minute' },
  { id: 'handoff', label: 'What you bring to the platform team' },
];
const installPrototype = `localcloud doctor
localcloud start --local-only --services cloudtasks,secretmanager,pubsub,gcs
eval "$(localcloud env)"`;
---

<BlogArticle
  slug="zero-ticket-cloud-prototyping"
  kind="Perspective"
  deck="You have an idea for how a service should work. Before you can try it you need a project, a billing link, IAM bindings and a sign-off. Try the idea first, on your own machine, and ask for infrastructure once you know what to ask for."
  primary={{ href: '/docs/', label: 'Get started' }}
  secondary={{ href: '/services/', label: 'Browse services' }}
  {contents}
  next={{ posts: ['single-container-gcp-beyond-three-emulators', 'validate-gcp-terraform-locally'], doc: { href: '/services/', label: 'Service catalog: every service and its ID' } }}
>
  <section>
    <h2 id="tuesday">An idea on Tuesday, access on Friday</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">On Tuesday you realise the retry loop in your worker should be a Cloud Tasks queue. It is an afternoon's experiment. You open a ticket for a sandbox project, and the experiment starts on Friday, if the approver isn't on holiday.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">Nobody in that chain is doing anything wrong. Platform teams gate projects because projects cost money and widen the attack surface. The trouble is the ordering: the request has to be made before anyone knows whether the idea is any good, so most small ideas never get tried.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">New starters meet the same wall on day one. The repository is cloned, the laptop works, and the application can't run because the cloud access hasn't come through.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="order">Reverse the order</h2>
    <table class="blog-compare mt-5">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Ticket first</th><th scope="col">Prototype first</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Day one</th><td>File the request and wait</td><td>Run the idea against local services</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">What the request says</th><td>"We might need Cloud Tasks"</td><td>"Here is the queue config and the tests that pass"</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">What gets provisioned</th><td>A broad sandbox, just in case</td><td>The resources the design uses</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">If the idea was wrong</th><td>A project to clean up</td><td>Nothing to undo</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="mt-4">LocalCloud starts with a project and a caller identity already in place, so there is nothing to request. The services answer the standard Google Cloud SDKs, which means the prototype is real code, the same code you would deploy.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="try-it">A working project in a minute</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">With the CLI installed from the <a href="/docs/#install-the-cli">quick start</a>, name the services the idea needs:</p>
    <BlogCode label="Start the services for the prototype" code={installPrototype} copy="copy-blog-zero-ticket-setup" />
    <p class="mt-4">Now compare the options with running code. Put a rate limit on a <a href="/services/cloud-tasks/">Cloud Tasks</a> queue and see how it behaves next to a <a href="/services/pubsub/">Pub/Sub</a> push subscription. Trigger the job from <a href="/services/cloud-scheduler/">Cloud Scheduler</a> or chain it in <a href="/services/cloud-workflows/">Cloud Workflows</a>. An afternoon gets you an answer.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">The same three commands belong in your README. A new engineer who can run them has the whole backend on their laptop before their access request is approved.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="handoff">What you bring to the platform team</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">The ticket still gets filed. It just reads differently. You attach the Terraform for exactly the resources the design needs and an integration suite that already passes against them. The reviewer is looking at a specific, tested change, which is an easier thing to approve than a guess.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">Platform teams tend to like this arrangement once they see it. Fewer speculative sandboxes to track, fewer permissions granted for experiments, and requests that arrive with their own evidence.</p>
  </section>
</BlogArticle>
